orin instead waits in the forest around  their farm until the sickness that took  his mother takes his stepfather as well  from what's said in the paramount plus  show it's unclear how close sorin's new  story is to pariah all he says to quan  in episode 4 is that it took him a year  after his defection to remember that his  father was dead and another year after  that to remember that he killed him  regardless of the details though the  halo show is clearly taking some  inspiration from pariah when it comes to  soren's past  the warthog is unquestionably the most  recognizable of all the halo vehicles  having appeared in one form or another  in every game in the franchise the most  common model of warthog is a minigun  mounted in the bed which a standing  marine can operate independently of the  driver in halo episode 4 another version  of the warthog is shown one that  features an expanded crew bed instead of  a mounted weapon master chief halsey and  her assistant used the vehicle during  the expedition to a readiness 2 where  they search for 4runner artifacts from  john's past this larger less combat  oriented warthog is drivable in various  halo games and it's one of many less  common variants one of the best things  about the paramount plus series is its  loyalty to the games in terms of visual  design for weapons vehicles and gear and  that stays true with the various  warthogs seen in halo  during a conversation with miranda keys  in episode 4 kai 125 invokes the name of  preston cole a pivotal figure from the  halo novels  from the age of six i studied military  strategy from sun zoo to preston coal  coal is referenced earlier when the coal  protocol a directive to keep the  location of earth from the covenant at  all costs is mentioned but it's a nice  touch for those who've read the books to  hear the admiral's full name cole's life  is explored in detail through various  halo books and comics including one of  the primary stories in halo evolutions  the 