s explaining  in her discussion of the needler she  drops a couple explicit references to  the games the gun comes from as soon as  the needles hit the target they super  combine and bam  all you see is a big pink mist of blood  and evaporating body fluids  the word super combine is a direct  callback to the games and the mention of  a pink mist is a reference to a  needler-based achievement in halo 3  which itself is a reference to the war  movie jarhead later in the scene as the  spartans help miranda decode the sangili  language kai can also be seen holding a  purple plasma grenade it's one of the  two original grenade types that are  usable in the original halo and it's the  most common such weapon wielded by the  covenant throughout the series there are  a number of other touches from the game  to miranda's lab such as the cadaver  pieces of sangili elites and what appear  to be dormant legolo in a tube the  worm-like creatures that combine to form  the fearsome covenant hunters  halo episode 5 reckoning takes place  almost entirely on master chief's  homeworld of aridness 2 where dr halsey  sets up a temporary camp to examine and  excavate the second 4runner keystone  the main ship in use for the operation  is the unsc pioneer and while it barely  even appears on screen in the paramount  plus show that name is pretty important  in the halo novels in halo the fall of  reach the pioneer is the ship the master  chief and his spartan two compatriots  take on a mission to you guessed it  aridness ii  in the canon of the books the planet and  its surrounding system are a hotbed of  rebel and insurrectionist activity  against the unsc and john and his team  are sent to apprehend a marine defector  operating in the area the tv series  references this rebellious history on  aridness 2 during the flashback when a  woman is showing halsey and captain  jacob keys around the planet's human  colony the colonial insurrection touched  everyone even here on our little out of  the way planet  of course in the canon of the show  aridness 2 is far less central to the  rebellion storyline but it's interesting  to see its original version referenced  by the use of the unsc pioneer halsey  and key's false pretenses for visiting  the planet are also pulled straight from  the old canon where they posed as a  couple to investigate potential spartans  before they ever actually got together  the aridness 2 battle in episode 5  features quite a few covenant species  and ships not previously or only barely  seen before in the series  the large phantom drop ships and smaller  banshee aircraft get a lot more screen  time in reckoning and they're joined by  several other iconic vehicles and  weapons the covenant deploy from their  cruiser via drop pods which are  incredibly faithful to their original  design seen in the games cortana even  refers to the small groups of aliens  held in the pods as lances the game's  term for a squad of covenant soldiers  later a different sort of dropship a  covenant spirit descends from the main  cruiser the distinct two-prong design of  the ship should be instantly familiar to  those who played either the original  halo or halo reach and other variations  of the ship appear across the franchise  it's fun to see the show's creators  pulling inspiration from so many  different corners of the lore especially  when iconic pieces of text such as the  spirit are recreated with such care  prior to halo episode 5 only the cengili  elites the prophets and the let golo are  seen representing the covenant that all  changes during the battle on aridness 2  which sees silver team in the unsc  marines facing off against a platoon of  jackals and grunts in the games these  two species are generally used as lower  level enemies under the command of  larger more tactical covenant officers  however as the halo series demonstrates  they can also be fearsome foes in their  own right for the most part the show  stays pretty close to the grunts and  jackals of the games they still  primarily use needlers and plasma  pistols and their visual design is  generally loyal there are some notable  differences however for instance one of  the jackals is seen dispatching marines  with a smaller version of an energy  sword a weapon typically only used by  elites in the games but there are  instances of jackals wielding similar  weapons in some of the halo books so  it's possible that this is another  reference for die hard fans while grunts  and jackals will never be the most  challenging or exciting enemies in halo  it's fun to see the show diving deeper  into the covenant ranks  as you might expect the halo show makes  a lot of winks and nods to its video  game origins some of these easter eggs  like master chief shield sound effects  and the first person perspective shots  are direct references to mechanics from  the halo games but some of them point to  other popular franchises or moments from  gaming history for instance in halo  episode 1 a voice can be heard over a  unsc loudspeaker saying commander  shepard you are requested at the  skillion response center in the popular  sci-fi game series mass effect commander  shepard is the main character in a  military operation known as the skillion  blitz as a key part of his backstory  during the battle on aridness 2 in halo  episode 5 cortana gives master chief a  good halo gameplay tip switch to  secondary i know how the game is played  this is a clear reference to the  traditional two weapon system used by  many first person shooter games  including halo  most halo games follow a similar pattern  as far as level design goes there's  always a tank level where you drive a  unsc scorpion and often a big warthog  escape sequence during the final chapter  and there's always at least one or two  bigger vehicle based levels halo episode  5 emulates that last archetype in its  climactic battle on a redness in which  master chief drives a warthog through  covenant ranks with his fellow spartans  banning the guns later on chief also  manages to jump onto a low-flying  banshee which he uses to destroy a  phantom drop ship in both cases master  chief effectively hijacks the vehicles  from covenant soldiers he shoots a grunt  out of the driver's seat of the warthog  and he tries his best to disrupt the  flight control of the banshee's pilot  after jumping aboard hijacking vehicles  is a major mechanic in the video games  and it especially comes into play in the  more open levels in multiplayer where  players are constantly jumping from one  motor transport to another  after finding his way through lances of  grunts jackals and banshees in halo  episode 5 master chief looks like he's  about to get the 4runner keystone safely  into the hands of the unsc but just when  it seems that all covenant resistance  has been defeated jon comes face to face  with one of the most dangerous soldiers  in the entire alien army a brute  chieftain  those who played the games will be  intimately familiar with the brutes  ape-like bruisers who serve as  lieutenants and front-line muscle for  the covenant the most dangerous brutes  are classified as chieftains and that  select group wields a range of  particularly powerful weapons in halo  episode 5 the chieftain who confronts  master chief holds the infamous gravity  hammer a massive melee weapon that  creates powerful shock waves each time  it hits the ground  throughout the halo tv show different  members of the covenant are heard  referring to master chief as demon a  nickname also heard in the video games  both the sangili and the prophets on  high charity use that term and mckee  even calls master chief demon to his  face when she finally meets him in  episode six  i'm john  you're the demon  that's what the sangheili called you  in the games chief is referred to as  demon in both cut scenes and combat for  instance when the player successfully  routes a group of grunts during a  firefight they'll often run screaming  about the demon and give up trying to  fight the name is also used by tougher  covenant soldiers like the brutes and  elites though they're usually less  frightened to master chief when they use  it  relax  i'd rather not piss this thing off demon  really the demon nickname is a testament  to how much master chief stands out  compared to the other spartans all of  halsey's soldiers receive the same  augmentations and training but john  rises head and shoulders above the rest  even the covenant themselves recognize  how exceptional he is on the battlefield  which is why they gave him the title of  demon  after conning her way into unsc custody  in halo episode 6 mckee tells master  chief that the covenant will be taking  the second forerunner keystone to a  planet called ross costa which is in the  espero system a sparrow is a name that a  few fans may recognize as it plays a  crucial role in a couple of the halo  novels in the original canon the espero  system is home to the planet hezduros an  old forerunner colony primarily  inhabited by singily in the present-day  timeline more importantly hezeros is  linked via a portal system to the  forerunner shield world of onix the  connection comes into play a couple of  times in the halo books including in  karen travis's halo the thursday war and  matt forback's halo legacy of onyx it  seems that the asparal system of the  live-action halo series is a bit  different roskoska doesn't figure into  the old lore in any major way and since  the character of mckee is brand new for  the show as well it makes sense that the  story is going in a different direction  a brief scene in the middle of halo  episode 6 shows kai entering a munitions  room as her comrades are looking at a  digital image of the m6 spartan laser in  the video games the spartan laser is one  of the most powerful weapons that  players can use but it also comes with a  downside to fire the bright red energy  blast produced by the weapon you must  first charge it for a few seconds this  caveat makes aiming the shot much more  challenging and landing one far more  satisfying the physical design of the  weapon is spot on in the paramount plus  show but the easter egg goes a bit  deeper by also referencing how the  spartan laser works was the discharge  rate on an m6c battery cell 20  right 20 so maximum five shocks  in halo episode 7 inheritance outer  colony mobster an all-around villain  venture grath is heard reciting some  darkly poetic verses about the planet  magical though the fuel harvested from  within the planet has made him a rich  ally to the unsc venture clearly doesn't  hold much love in his heart for the  planet nor for those who fought him to  defend it the poetry recited by venture  is actually pulled directly from the  john milton classic paradise lost and  that specific selection is of special  relevance to the story of halo in the  most literal sense venture's choice of  poem signifies how he views magical as a  hellscape he's been unfairly cast down  into the section he quotes details the  ordeal of satan and the other fallen  angels of heaven who are cast down into  the fires of hell to suffer after  rebelling against god it's curious that  venture would choose to self-identify as  the devil in this instance but maybe  that's just what bad guys do  after returning to madrigal in hopes of  reigniting her father's rebellion kwon  ha journeys deep into the planet's  barren desert and discovers a remote  enclave of mystics she's informed that  the group is connected to her ancestors  and that her father learned important  secrets from them we didn't start with  your father  there were others in your family before  him  in the hopes of following in his  footsteps kwon undergoes a hallucinatory  journey of the mind by ingesting some  mysterious liquid provided by the  mystics  anyone who's read frank herbert's dune  or seen any of the live-action  adaptations will immediately recognize  some inspiration here the classic sci-fi  novel revolves around the remote desert  planet of arrakis which is valued  because of the addictive and  mind-altering spice melange that grows  there many other movies shows video  games and books have pulled elements  from doom and the halo series continues  that tradition  quanta's journey into the magical desert  leads her to a curious and exciting  revelation there's a portal within the  planet that her ancestors have fought to  protect for generations during her  drug-induced dream sequence quan  glimpses a well dug by one of her  forefathers out of which flies a small  robotic being those who play the halo  games will instantly recognize the  creature as a monitor an autonomous  machine created by the forerunners to  oversee and protect their various  installations the most famous monitor in  the halo video games is 343 guilty spark  a deceptive character who at different  points serves as both friend and foe  presumably the monitor scene in the  paramount plus series journeys to  madrigal through the portal within the  planet which may connect it to one of  the halo rings forerunner portals of  this kind also have a precedent in halo  games and books and there have been  multiple instances of important  locations being linked with such  technology  after completing her vision quest quan  has informed that she is a protector  someone called to defend the secrets of  mandrigold the particular word used is  interesting because in the halo games  protector is a subset to the 4runner  sentinel robots that guard the halo  rings and other similar installations  because of the magical portal's  connection to the forerunners this could  be an intentional nod to the games  bringing the humans who guard the portal  under the same banner as the sentinels  themselves either way quan quickly  proves her salts as a protector by  taking down venture graft and a whole  squadron of his men she scores one  particularly gruesome kill in classic  halo fashion by sticking a covenant  plasma grenade to the back of one of her  enemies if we're going to fight  we meet them on my terms  one of the sillier easter eggs of halo  season 1 comes early in episode 8  allegiance when master chief and mckee  go for a walk in the park on reach  while out and about the two stop and  chat in front of an unassuming lemonade  stand the logo on the employee's uniform  and the stand itself advertises goss  lemonade if you played the latter halo  games you may recognize the brand name  halo 5 guardians and halo infinite both  feature references to a fictional canned  drink called goss which uses slogans in  its advertisements like drink like a  spartan and kick your tongue in the face  the name of the drink seems to be a  reference to the goss rifle itself named  for fame physicist carl friedrich goss  which is a commonly seen mounted weapon  on warthogs and halo games  while talking to master chief and  allegiance mckee quotes a bit of an  ancient covenant prophecy pertaining to  the supposed great journey when the  stars sparkle like glass a divine wind  will rush through propelling all those  who are worthy on a great journey to the  sacred ring where we will become like  gods the verse references a brutal  covenant war tactic that comes into play  later in the episode glassing in the  halo games glassine is the term used for  the alien army's genocidal attacks on  planets where powerful energy weapons  are used to melt everything on the  surface shortly after mckee invokes the  term the unsc is brought face to face  with just such an attack the glass  planet is revealed to be none other than  criterion a human colony from the halo  books in the original version of the  story the planet is defended by the unsc  in a full-scale battle unfortunately  criterion isn't so lucky in the tv show  after seeing the decimation wrought by  the covenant on criterion admiral  margaret parangoski urgently request  that someone named admiral whitcomb be  contacted in an effort to launch a  counter-attack though the plan is vetoed  by captain jacob keys those who've read  halo first strike or halo ghost of onyx  may recognize the name admiral danforth  whitcomb is a prominent character in the  first strike novel in particular whereas  many military exploits and successful  missions against a covenant are detailed  wickham was one of the highest ranking  and most decorated officers in the unsc  during the human covenant war and it's  fun to see him getting a little shout  out in the live-action halo show  in the final episode of halo season 1  master chief leads silver team on a  dangerous mission to the covenant's holy  planet where mckee and the prophets are  preparing to unite the two keystones and  signal a path to the halo ring what  follows is an extended action sequence  which begins when chief leads his squad  in an orbital drop from their ship down  to the planet's surface the camera then  jumps to the ground where covenant grunt  is snoozing on guard duty his nap ends  up being the death of him as master  chief crashes into him from the sky  before the grunt can even wake up a  notice in the halo games sleeping grunts  are a common sight many levels feature  whole squads of the little guys napping  frighteningly close to a combat zone  master chief's battle to procure the  4runner keystones in the season 1 finale  of halo pits him and the rest of the  silver team against a mighty covenant  force when the prophets discover the  humans invasion they summon the zealots  a subset of zengili elites that fans of  the games will be well acquainted with  however the zealots of the paramount  plus halo series are quite different  than those found in the source material  historically zealots have been the most  elite of the elites commanders and  leaders equipped with powerful weapons  and armor in fact the ceremonial guard  who performs the keystone ceremony looks  more like those original z